1,"sym":{"clay":70,"jackson":62,"ray":61,"lafayette":75,"daviess":21,"buchanan":17},"gang":0,"cover":52},{"t":32,"purse":19394,"legend":88,"reward":7297,"pinkerton":16,"heat":1,"sym":{"clay":71,"jackson":62,"ray":61,"lafayette":75,"daviess":22,"buchanan":18},"gang":0,"cover":53},{"t":33,"purse":19174,"legend":86,"reward":7261,"pinkerton":16,"heat":1,"sym":{"clay":71,"jackson":62,"ray":61,"lafayette":75,"daviess":23,"buchanan":19},"gang":0,"cover":79}],"dead":[{"id":"frank","name":"Frank James","t":10,"how":"killed on the run, First National Bank, Kansas City"}],"stats":{"jobs":18,"take":64284,"cashiers":0,"bystanders":0,"failed":0,"letters":6},"report":{"t":33,"title":"Mr. Thomas Howard, Nashville, Tennessee","job":null,"events":[]},"log":[{"t":0,"kind":"story","text":"The war is over. Not for you. Quantrill is dead, Bloody Bill is dead, and the men who rode with them cannot go home to vote, preach or practise law. Some of them still have their horses."},{"t":1,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,839 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":2,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,981 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":3,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: BOLD ROBBERY — $3,320 Carried Off"},{"t":4,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: A QUIET ROBBERY — $3,377 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":5,"kind":"job","text":"GALLATIN: A QUIET ROBBERY — $2,076 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":6,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $639 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":7,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: A QUIET ROBBERY — $573 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":8,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: A QUIET ROBBERY — $3,353 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":9,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: A QUIET ROBBERY — $833 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":10,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: BOLD ROBBERY — $6,171 Carried Off — Posse in Pursuit"},{"t":11,"kind":"bad","text":"Someone in Clay County wanted the reward more than they feared you. You get out the back with your boots in your hand."},{"t":12,"kind":"job","text":"GALLATIN: A QUIET ROBBERY — $2,414 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":13,"kind":"good","text":"In Lafayette they will say you paid a widow's mortgage. Sympathy +8."},{"t":14,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $5,915 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":16,"kind":"history","text":"John Newman Edwards of the Kansas City Times, who rode with Jo Shelby, has started printing letters from Missouri's \"outlaws\" and defending them as Southern men driven to it. He will print yours. (After a job you can write to him: legend and Southern sympathy rise, and so does the reward.)","title":"A friend at the Kansas City Times"},{"t":16,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,704 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":17,"kind":"news","text":"John Newman Edwards prints your letter: you are a Southern man wronged by Radicals, and you name the men who can swear where you were that day."},{"t":18,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $9,000 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":19,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":20,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: A QUIET ROBBERY — $2,507 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":21,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":22,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: A QUIET ROBBERY — $3,466 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":23,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":24,"kind":"bad","text":"Someone in Clay County wanted the reward more than they feared you. You get out the back with your boots in your hand."},{"t":24,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $2,695 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":25,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":26,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: A QUIET ROBBERY — $3,421 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":27,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":28,"kind":"history","text":"The railroads are across Missouri now and the express companies ship cash in locked safes in the baggage cars. A train can be stopped like a stage, if you pull up a rail. Train jobs are open.","title":"The express cars"},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Cole Younger takes his stake home."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Jim Younger takes his stake home."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Jim Cummins takes his stake home."},{"t":29,"kind":"bad","text":"A man who rode with Quantrill knew your face in a feed store. You get out the back with your boots in your hand."},{"t":29,"kind":"bad","text":"Mr. Howard is finished. You will have to start again somewhere else."},{"t":32,"kind":"history","text":"The Adams Express Company has hired the Pinkerton National Detective Agency of Chicago to hunt you. They do not give up, they do not mind whom they hurt, and they pay informers.","title":"The Pinkertons"},{"t":32,"kind":"warn","text":"A detective has been asking at the boarding houses about men who pay in gold. Your landlady mentioned it."},{"t":33,"kind":"history","text":"You marry Zee Mimms, your cousin, after nine years of courting. Frank says you are a fool. He is at the wedding.","title":"A wedding"}]}